Z||000000||Shiner||01-26-2000||01:12 AM||||I thought you guys would get a huge kick out of this one!  It was posted on the fiero list some time ago. (I apologize for it's size, but it's really worth it!)<P><BR>Now that the car had body mods, I thought that custom wheels were in order.  I contacted a shop here in town and bought a set of 17 inch Avanti Racing Hearts.  I didn’t realize until I got the stock wheels off that I bought the wrong bolt pattern.  Using a drill bit duct taped to the blades of an upside down food processor I attempted to drill another hole in one of the wheels but wound up cracking it.  The shop in town <BR>said that they would not replace the set even though my mom yelled at them over the phone.  She was pretty ticked, but I found an alternative to wheels and I will get to that later.<P>When I had the wheels off the car I could see the brakes.  I originally thought that the brakes were in the engine (because they slow the car down) but for those of you that don’t know, there is one at each wheel.  <BR>I once saw rotors with hole in them and it looked cool so I decided to put some holes in mine.  Again using the ‘food processor as a drill’ method, I cross drilled my rotors.  it took a long time, and one rotor <BR>cracked (I fixed it using JB Weld)  they look cool now.  Maybe someone on the list can look into why cross drilling rotors is done and if it is of any benefit other than aesthetic.  I also painted my springs.  First yellow but the watercolour paint came off too easy, so I painted them red using an oil based paint.<P>While doing the brake and suspension (springs) work, I had been buying as many 1984 deck lids as I could find.  Using the slotted screwdriver and ball peen hammer  I carved a circle 17 inches across, 9 inched deep into the sidewalk beside my house.  Next, I removed the magnesium grilles from the 1984 deck lid and melted them down until the hole was filled.  I did this four times, resulting in some pretty cool magnesium <BR>wheels for my project car.  I managed to stretch the tires from the stock 15s around my magnesium wheels, so it looks really low profile.  I mounted them to the hub with solder and JB weld so they will hold tight.<P>A few years ago when Mom and I lived in a trailer park there as a hurricane, and a lot of stuff got blown around.  One thing that was blown a lot was aluminium siding.   Since I had some sitting around I wondered how it could be used on my car (since titled “Fast DreamCar1”).  A new spoiler was needed to keep the rear end down with all of the extra horsepower flying around because of the new air intakes.  I couldn’t get the old wing off so I cut it off on each side of each stantion and then pried the stantions off the deck lid with my screwdriver.  Using copper pipe (you know where that came from) I fabricated new stantions and soldered them to the wing.  Then I used my hammer and hit the new stantions through the deck lid.  the holes aren’t very uniform, but the wing is very functional.<P>My car was roadready and I almost announced it to the list.  but I learned about carbon fibre interior kits.  I didn’t know what carbon fibre was, so I talked to mom about it.  It turns out it’s a super strong and lightweight material that can be formed into almost any shape.  I KNEW AT THAT POINT I WANTED A CARBON FIBRE BODY!  I contacted <BR>some suppliers around town and learned that carbon fibre is not only hard to work with, but very expensive.  In fact, the only place it is used extensively in automotive applications is in F-1 and CART  bodies.  <BR>Carbon fibre had to become a part of my project, so I found an alternative.<P>3M makes a glue that some in a spray can.  I sprayed the entire body of Fast DreamCar 1 with glue and covered it with burlap (yes, burlap potato bags) to mimic carbon fibres texture.  I wasn’t stupid and made sure to <BR>cut holes for doors, windows and deck lids before applying the burlap. When the glue dried I sprayed the entire car with matte black paint,again added to the authenticity of the carbon fibre body.<P>I have been able to start the car but it has not left the garage yet. Tomorrow will be it’s first day on the road and I will let the list know how it goes.  I plan to take best of show at Carlisle and Daytona this <BR>year so remember,DON’T MESS WITH BENJAMIN LONG OR FAST DREAMCAR 1!<P>I will G-tech the car and post the results to the list (expected 400+hp)<P>Benjamin F. Long,<BR>Founder of F.O.R.M.<BR>Fiero Owners for the Re-Birth of the Machine.<BR>||||reg||6
